  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nThrough tonight: Another day of much warmer than normal temperatures \nis expected today before cooler temperatures move into night in the \nwake of a cold front. Early morning satellite imagery/RAP analysis \nshowed the circulation around a shortwave trough over the central \nand southern plains that will continue swinging east and help \nsupport a cold front move into the northwest half of our CWA by \nsunrise Monday morning. Early morning surface analysis still had a \nridge across our CWA from the northeast and the cold front \nstretching across eastern Kansas, central Oklahoma and into central \nTexas. The surface ridge will weaken today but the increase in \nmoisture ahead of the cold front will remain quite limited with \nsurface dewpoints only rising into the lower 60s over our southwest \nwith PWATs an inch and a quarter. Convection along and just ahead of \nthe cold front will begin moving into the northwest portions of our \nCWA late this afternoon. Most of the convection will be in the form \nof showers but a few storms will be possible. This activity will \nweaken as it sags towards the Natchez Trace corridor by morning. \nRainfall totals across our northwest half of the CWA are expected to \nbe around a tenth of an inch on average. Highs this afternoon will \nbe in the upper 70s to near 80F. Morning lows Monday will range from \nthe upper 40s northwest to the upper 50s southeast. /22/\n\nMonday - Saturday\n\nCooler temperatures will usher in the new week as a cold front exit \nthe area late Monday. Rain chances will gradually decrease through \nthe morning, leaving below seasonal temperature in its wake. \nHighs/lows will run 5-10 degrees cooler throughout the week. Rain \nchances (25-60%) return on Wednesday as a shortwave progress over \nthe area. Unfortunately, scare rainfall totals (0.30 inches or less) \nare expected and ongoing drought conditions will continue. Dry and \nquiet weather will return for Thanksgiving and linger into the \nweekend. /SW/",
